### The problem hexagonal architecture solves in a Nest codebase

The default NestJS tutorial style couples business logic directly to the framework. You get an `@Injectable()` service with `@InjectRepository(Order)` injected straight in. Business rules then sit next to calls into the object-relational mapper (ORM), in the same method.

For create-read-update-delete (CRUD) apps that is fine. For a domain with real business rules it is not. You cannot unit test a pricing rule without starting up, or heavily mocking, TypeORM. And swapping the ORM, or adding a second way in — a command line tool, a gRPC endpoint — means touching the business logic itself.

Hexagonal architecture's core move: the domain layer defines **ports**, which are interfaces for what it needs from the outside world. Framework-specific code lives entirely in **adapters** that implement those ports. The domain never imports anything from `@nestjs/*` or `typeorm`.

```typescript
// domain/order.ts — pure TypeScript, zero framework imports
export class Order {
  private constructor(
    public readonly id: string,
    private items: OrderItem[],
    private status: OrderStatus,
  ) {}

  static create(id: string, items: OrderItem[]): Order {
    if (items.length === 0) throw new EmptyOrderError();
    return new Order(id, items, OrderStatus.Draft);
  }

  ship(): void {
    if (this.status !== OrderStatus.Paid) {
      throw new InvalidOrderStateError('Cannot ship unpaid order');
    }
    this.status = OrderStatus.Shipped;
  }
}

// domain/ports/order-repository.port.ts — the port the domain depends on
export interface OrderRepositoryPort {
  save(order: Order): Promise<void>;
  findById(id: string): Promise<Order | null>;
}
// injection token, still framework-agnostic as a plain symbol
export const ORDER_REPOSITORY = Symbol('ORDER_REPOSITORY');

// application/create-order.use-case.ts — orchestration, testable without Nest
export class CreateOrderUseCase {
  // depends on the interface, not on TypeORM
  constructor(private readonly orders: OrderRepositoryPort) {}
  async execute(id: string, items: OrderItem[]): Promise<void> {
    const order = Order.create(id, items);
    await this.orders.save(order);
  }
}
```

### Where NestJS actually enters the picture

The **adapter** layer is where `@Injectable()`, `@InjectRepository()` and everything Nest-specific live. An adapter implements the port with framework tools. It is wired through a custom provider, so the injection token stays the abstract port symbol rather than the concrete class:

```typescript
// infrastructure/typeorm-order.repository.ts — adapter over TypeORM
@Injectable()
export class TypeOrmOrderRepository implements OrderRepositoryPort {
  constructor(
    @InjectRepository(OrderEntity) private repo: Repository<OrderEntity>,
  ) {}
  async save(order: Order): Promise<void> {
    /* map domain Order -> OrderEntity, then repo.save(...) */
  }
  async findById(id: string): Promise<Order | null> {
    /* repo.findOne, then map back to a domain Order */
  }
}

@Module({
  providers: [
    CreateOrderUseCase, // Nest instantiates this fine without a decorator
    { provide: ORDER_REPOSITORY, useClass: TypeOrmOrderRepository },
  ],
})
export class OrdersModule {}
```

This buys two concrete things. First, `CreateOrderUseCase` and `Order` are testable with plain Jest: no Nest testing module, no database, just a hand-written fake `OrderRepositoryPort`. Second, swapping TypeORM for Prisma, or adding a Kafka consumer alongside the HTTP controller, touches only the adapter layer.

**Common pitfall:** letting request objects with `class-validator` decorators such as `@IsString()` and `@IsNotEmpty()` leak into the domain layer, as if they were domain objects. Those decorators validate what arrived over HTTP, so they belong on the adapter layer's data transfer objects. A domain entity should enforce its invariants in its own factory, the way `Order.create()` does above.

