### What a trace gives you that scattered logs never can

A correlation id in log lines lets you `grep` for related entries across services. But rebuilding the actual call tree from grep output is manual work. You have to work out who called whom, how long each hop took, and where the time went across five hops.

A **trace** is that call tree as structured data. There is a root span for the initial request and a child span for every downstream call, each with start and end timestamps. The result is a tree you can visualize, in Jaeger or Tempo, and see at once which hop in a five-service chain added 400ms.

```typescript
// tracing.ts — instrumented once at bootstrap, not per request
const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  traceExporter: new OTLPTraceExporter({
    url: 'http://otel-collector:4318/v1/traces',
  }),
  // auto-instruments http, express, some database clients
  instrumentations: [getNodeAutoInstrumentations()],
});
sdk.start();
```

Auto-instrumentation covers HTTP and common libraries out of the box. But Nest's microservice transports are not standard HTTP — neither the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) transport nor the Kafka `ClientProxy`. They need explicit span creation and context propagation at the transport boundary. That is the part that takes real understanding, not just installing a package.

### How context crosses a TCP or Kafka boundary

A trace's identity — trace id plus parent span id — has to travel *with* the message. The receiving service has no other way to know which trace it belongs to. The OpenTelemetry convention is to inject that identity into message headers, in the `traceparent` format defined by the W3C (World Wide Web Consortium). The receiving side extracts it before creating a child span:

```typescript
// sending side — inject trace context into the message metadata
async callInventory(payload: any) {
  const span = tracer.startSpan('inventory.reserve');
  const carrier: Record<string, string> = {};
  // writes traceparent into carrier
  propagation.inject(trace.setSpan(context.active(), span), carrier);

  return firstValueFrom(
    this.inventoryClient.send('reserve-inventory', { ...payload, _trace: carrier }),
  ).finally(() => span.end());
}

// receiving side — extract and continue the same trace as a child span
@MessagePattern('reserve-inventory')
handleReserve(@Payload() data: any) {
  const parentContext = propagation.extract(context.active(), data._trace);
  return context.with(parentContext, () => {
    const span = tracer.startSpan('inventory.handle-reserve'); // child span
    try {
      return this.inventoryService.reserve(data);
    } finally {
      span.end();
    }
  });
}
```

Without this explicit inject and extract at every non-HTTP hop, each service's spans exist in isolation. You get per-service traces that never link into one end-to-end tree. That defeats the point of distributed tracing: you are back to correlating disconnected fragments by hand.

### What spans encode that a log line structurally cannot

A span has a duration, not a single timestamp: it records both start and end. It has a parent-child relationship to other spans. And it carries structured attributes such as `db.statement` and `http.status_code`, queryable across the whole set of traces.

That turns one question into a real query: "show me every trace where the `inventory.reserve` span took over 200ms". The alternative is reading timestamps off scattered log lines and subtracting them by hand.

**Common pitfall:** instrumenting only inbound HTTP controllers and assuming the trace is complete. Every outbound `ClientProxy.send()` then starts a brand-new, disconnected trace on the receiving side, because context propagation was never wired into the transport layer. The dashboard shows dozens of tiny one-hop traces per request instead of the single end-to-end trace you were paying for.

