### Why the default in-memory storage silently breaks under horizontal scaling

The default `ThrottlerStorage` in `@nestjs/throttler` keeps a request counter per client key, in the process's own memory. The key is an IP (internet protocol) address or a user id. For a single instance that is correct.

Run 3 replicas behind a load balancer and each instance tracks its own independent counter. A client on a "10 requests per minute" limit can then make up to 30 per minute in total. Requests round-robin across the 3 instances, and each one believes the client is well under the limit.

This is not a rare edge case. It is the default, guaranteed behaviour of any horizontally scaled deployment with in-memory throttling.

```typescript
@Module({
  imports: [
    ThrottlerModule.forRoot({
      // a per-instance limit if storage isn't shared — the actual bug
      throttlers: [{ ttl: 60000, limit: 10 }],
    }),
  ],
})
export class AppModule {}
```

### A Redis-backed ThrottlerStorage centralizes the counter

`@nestjs/throttler` accepts a custom `ThrottlerStorage` implementation through `storage:`. Swapping in a Redis-backed one makes the counter shared and atomic across every instance:

```typescript
@Injectable()
export class RedisThrottlerStorage implements ThrottlerStorage {
  constructor(@InjectRedis() private redis: Redis) {}

  async increment(key: string, ttl: number): Promise<ThrottlerStorageRecord> {
    const multi = this.redis.multi();
    multi.incr(key);
    multi.pttl(key);
    const results = await multi.exec();
    const totalHits = results[0][1] as number;
    let timeToExpire = results[1][1] as number;

    if (totalHits === 1 || timeToExpire === -1) {
      // set the expiry only on the first hit in the window
      await this.redis.pexpire(key, ttl);
      timeToExpire = ttl;
    }
    return { totalHits, timeToExpire };
  }
}

ThrottlerModule.forRootAsync({
  inject: ['REDIS_CLIENT'],
  useFactory: (redis: Redis) => ({
    throttlers: [{ ttl: 60000, limit: 10 }],
    storage: new RedisThrottlerStorage(redis),
  }),
});
```

`INCR` plus `PTTL` inside a single Redis `MULTI` is what makes this correct under concurrency. `INCR` increments and returns the new count atomically, in one round trip. So two requests from the same client, arriving at two different instances, still get their increments serialized by Redis itself. The alternative is each instance doing a read-then-write, which could let both through when only one should pass.

### The trade-off you are accepting

Centralizing the counter in Redis adds one network round trip per request that the in-memory version did not have. It also makes Redis availability a dependency of every rate-limited endpoint.

So you need an explicit decision for when Redis is unreachable: fail open and allow the request, or fail closed and reject it. Leaving an unhandled Redis error to crash the request path is not a decision. Fail-open is usually right here. A rate limiter exists to protect against abuse. It should not become a single point of failure that drops legitimate traffic when its own store fails.

**Common pitfall:** deploying a multi-instance service with the default in-memory `ThrottlerStorage` and believing the limit is enforced. Every local test and every single-replica staging run passes, because with one instance the counter is correct. The bug appears only under real horizontal scaling. And it never looks like a failing test — it looks like a client sailing past the limit.

